#{[17592186534080 :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{3\\times 2}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)=(Ax-b)^{T}(Ax-b),$ where\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" cellspacing=\"0\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" rowspan=\"3\">\n${∇}_{A}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td rowspan=\"3\" width=\"8px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"45px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"45px\">$0$</td>\n<td rowspan=\"3\" width=\"8px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\">$-32$</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$16$</td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186534080 :question/worked-solution "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWe can compute $\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}$ by applying the chain rule.\n\nLet $y=Ax-b\\in {R}^{3}.$ Then, $L(A)={y}^{T}y$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}={\\sum }_{k=1}^{3}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n{∇}_{y}L={∇}_{y}({y}^{T}y)=2y,\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}=2{y}_{k}.\n$$\n\nWriting out $y=Ax-b$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\n{y}_{i}={\\sum }_{j=1}^{2}{a}_{ij}{x}_{j}-{b}_{i},\n$$\n\nwe have \n\n- $\\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}={x}_{j}$ if $i=k$, and\n\n- $\\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}=0$ if $i\\ne k.$\n\nThis means that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}} & ={\\sum }_{k=1}^{3}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{i}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =2{y}_{i}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =2{y}_{i}{x}_{j}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe $(i,j)$th entry of ${∇}_{A}L$ is $2$ times the $i$th entry of $y$ multiplied by the $j$th entry of $x$, so\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=2y\\cdot {x}^{T}.\n$$\n\nFinally, evaluating ${∇}_{A}L$ at $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{A}L & =2y\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =2(Ax-b)\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =2(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0 \\\\ 3 & -2\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix})\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =2(\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ -4 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}8 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix})\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -8 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ -32 & -32 \\\\ 16 & 16\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186534082 :question/problem "Consider the function $L:{R}^{2\\times 3}\\to R$ defined by $L(A)=(2Ax-b)^{T}(2Ax-b),$ where\n\n$$\nx=\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix},\\,b=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nWhat is ${∇}_{A}L$ at \n$$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}?\n$$\n\n<table cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"70px\" rowspan=\"2\" width=\"30px\">\n${∇}_{A}=\\,\\,$\n</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"30px\">$-240$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"35px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-3}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-4}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\">$-2140$</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186534082 :question/worked-solution "The gradient of $L$ with respect to $A$ is a matrix of the same shape as $A$, with $(i,j)$th entry given by\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}},\n$$\n\nthe partial derivative of $L$ with respect to the $(i,j)$th entry of $A.$\n\nWe can compute $\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}$ by applying the chain rule.\n\nLet $y=2Ax-b\\in {R}^{2}.$ Then, $L(A)={y}^{T}y$ and\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}}={\\sum }_{k=1}^{2}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}\n$$\n\nNow, since \n\n$$\n{∇}_{y}L={∇}_{y}({y}^{T}y)=2y,\n$$\n\nwe have that\n\n$$\n\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}=2{y}_{k}.\n$$\n\nWriting out $y=2Ax-b$ in component-wise notation as \n\n$$\n{y}_{i}=2{\\sum }_{j=1}^{3}{a}_{ij}{x}_{j}-{b}_{i},\n$$\n\nwe have \n\n- $\\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}=2{x}_{j}$ if $i=k$, and\n\n- $\\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}}=0$ if $i\\ne k.$\n\nThis means that \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{∂L}{∂{a}_{ij}} & ={\\sum }_{k=1}^{2}\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{k}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{k}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =\\frac{∂L}{∂{y}_{i}}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =2{y}_{i}\\cdot \\frac{∂{y}_{i}}{∂{a}_{ij}} \\\\  & =2{y}_{i}\\cdot 2{x}_{j} \\\\  & =4{y}_{i}{x}_{j}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n The $(i,j)$th entry of ${∇}_{A}L$ is $4$ times the $i$th entry of $y$ multiplied by the $j$th entry of $x$, so\n\n$$\n{∇}_{A}L=4y\\cdot {x}^{T}.\n$$\n\n Finally, evaluating ${∇}_{A}L$ at $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{∇}_{A}L & =4y\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =4(2Ax-b)\\cdot {x}^{T} \\\\  & =4(2\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & 1 \\\\ 4 & 0 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 10 \\\\ -5\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix})\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}10 & 10 & -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =4(\\begin{bmatrix}10 \\\\ 110\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix})\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}10 & 10 & -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}48 \\\\ 428\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}10 & 10 & -5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}480 & 480 & -240 \\\\ 4280 & 4280 & -2140\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186534093 :question/problem "$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the system of linear ODEs ${x}^{′}=Ax$ with the matrix $A$ shown above. Compute the **standard** fundamental matrix ${Φ}_{0}(t)$ for this system.\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">${Φ}_{0}(t)=\\frac{1}{2}\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">${e}^{6t}-{e}^{-6t}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">${e}^{6t}-{e}^{-6t}$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"80px\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr></tbody></table>"] [17592186534093 :question/worked-solution "Suppose $A$ is a constant $n\\times n$ matrix and $x(t)$ is an $n\\times 1$ column vector. The standard fundamental matrix of the linear system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ denoted ${Φ}_{0}(t),$ is the unique matrix solution of the initial value problem \n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}^{′}(t)=A{Φ}_{0}(t),\\,{Φ}_{0}(0)={I}_{n},\n$$\n\nwhere ${I}_{n}$ is the identity matrix. \n\nFurthermore, it can be shown that ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}.$\n\nWe have a square matrix of dimension $n=2.$ Thus, \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{At} & ={\\alpha }_{1}At+{\\alpha }_{0}I \\\\  & ={\\alpha }_{1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 6 \\\\ 6 & 0\\end{bmatrix}t+{\\alpha }_{0}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{\\alpha }_{0} & 6{\\alpha }_{1}t \\\\ 6{\\alpha }_{1}t & {\\alpha }_{0}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ are constants to be determined.\n\nConsider the polynomial $r(\\lambda )={\\alpha }_{1}\\lambda +{\\alpha }_{0}.$ The eigenvalues of $At$ are \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}=-6t,\\,{\\lambda }_{2}=6t,\n$$\n\neach of multiplicity one. Thus, we have:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\{\\begin{aligned}r(-6t)={e}^{-6t} \\\\ r(6t)={e}^{6t}\\end{aligned}\\,⇒\\,\\{\\begin{aligned}-6t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-6t} \\\\ 6t{\\alpha }_{1}+{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{6t}\\end{aligned}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we solve the system for ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}:$\n\n- Subtracting the second equation from the first one, we get\n\n$$\n-12t{\\alpha }_{1}={e}^{-6t}-{e}^{6t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{1}=-\\frac{1}{12t}({e}^{-6t}-{e}^{6t}).\n$$\n\n- Adding the first and the second equations, we get\n\n$$\n2{\\alpha }_{0}={e}^{-6t}+{e}^{6t}\\,⇒\\,{\\alpha }_{0}=\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{-6t}+{e}^{6t}).\n$$\n\nSubstituting ${\\alpha }_{1}$ and ${\\alpha }_{0}$ into the expressions for the required entries of ${e}^{At},$ we get the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\alpha }_{0} & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{-6t}+{e}^{6t}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{6t}+{e}^{-6t}) \\\\ 6{\\alpha }_{1}t & =6\\cdot (-\\frac{1}{12t}({e}^{-6t}-{e}^{6t}))t \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}({e}^{6t}-{e}^{-6t})\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,\n\n$$\n{Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}=\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}{e}^{6t}+{e}^{-6t} & {e}^{6t}-{e}^{-6t} \\\\ {e}^{6t}-{e}^{-6t} & {e}^{6t}+{e}^{-6t}\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186534217 :question/problem "If $$\n\\displaystyle R=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 7 & -2 \\\\ -6 & 2 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 6 & 7\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ then $det(R)=${{field-1}}."] [17592186534217 :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 7 & -2 \\\\ -6 & 2 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 6 & 7\\end{vmatrix} & =8\\begin{vmatrix}2 & -6 \\\\ 6 & 7\\end{vmatrix}-7\\begin{vmatrix}-6 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 7\\end{vmatrix}+(-2)\\begin{vmatrix}-6 & 2 \\\\ -6 & 6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =8(2\\cdot 7-(-6)\\cdot 6)+(-7)((-6)\\cdot 7-(-6)\\cdot (-6))+(-2)((-6)\\cdot 6-2\\cdot (-6)) \\\\  & =8(14-(-36))+(-7)(-42-36)+(-2)(-36-(-12)) \\\\  & =8\\cdot 50+(-7)\\cdot (-78)+(-2)\\cdot (-24) \\\\  & =400+546+48 \\\\  & =994\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186534218 :question/problem "If $$\n\\displaystyle Q=\\begin{bmatrix}6 & -7 & 8 \\\\ 7 & 4 & 8 \\\\ 7 & -5 & 6\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ then $det(Q)=${{field-1}}."] [17592186534218 :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}6 & -7 & 8 \\\\ 7 & 4 & 8 \\\\ 7 & -5 & 6\\end{vmatrix} & =6\\begin{vmatrix}4 & 8 \\\\ -5 & 6\\end{vmatrix}-(-7)\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 8 \\\\ 7 & 6\\end{vmatrix}+8\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 4 \\\\ 7 & -5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =6(4\\cdot 6-8\\cdot (-5))+7(7\\cdot 6-8\\cdot 7)+8(7\\cdot (-5)-4\\cdot 7) \\\\  & =6(24-(-40))+7(42-56)+8(-35-28) \\\\  & =6\\cdot 64+7\\cdot (-14)+8\\cdot (-63) \\\\  & =384-98-504 \\\\  & =-218\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186534226 :question/problem "If $$\n\\displaystyle H=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 7 & 7 & 3 \\\\ 8 & 5 & -2\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ then $det(H)=${{field-1}}."] [17592186534226 :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}-7 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 7 & 7 & 3 \\\\ 8 & 5 & -2\\end{vmatrix} & =(-7)\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 3 \\\\ 5 & -2\\end{vmatrix}-2\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 3 \\\\ 8 & -2\\end{vmatrix}+(-2)\\begin{vmatrix}7 & 7 \\\\ 8 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-7)(7\\cdot (-2)-3\\cdot 5)+(-2)(7\\cdot (-2)-3\\cdot 8)+(-2)(7\\cdot 5-7\\cdot 8) \\\\  & =(-7)(-14-15)+(-2)(-14-24)+(-2)(35-56) \\\\  & =(-7)\\cdot (-29)+(-2)\\cdot (-38)+(-2)\\cdot (-21) \\\\  & =203+76+42 \\\\  & =321\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186534228 :question/problem "Find the value of $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}10 & -8 & -9 \\\\ -11 & 12 & -7 \\\\ 12 & 7 & 12\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186534228 :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}10 & -8 & -9 \\\\ -11 & 12 & -7 \\\\ 12 & 7 & 12\\end{vmatrix} & =10\\begin{vmatrix}12 & -7 \\\\ 7 & 12\\end{vmatrix}-(-8)\\begin{vmatrix}-11 & -7 \\\\ 12 & 12\\end{vmatrix}+(-9)\\begin{vmatrix}-11 & 12 \\\\ 12 & 7\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =10(144-(-49))+8(-132-(-84))+(-9)(-77-144) \\\\  & =10\\cdot 193+8\\cdot (-48)+(-9)\\cdot (-221) \\\\  & =1930-384+1989 \\\\  & =3535\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186534229 :question/problem "Find the value of $$\n\\displaystyle \\begin{vmatrix}2 & 8 & -6 \\\\ -7 & 5 & -8 \\\\ 3 & -6 & 3\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186534229 :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}2 & 8 & -6 \\\\ -7 & 5 & -8 \\\\ 3 & -6 & 3\\end{vmatrix} & =2\\begin{vmatrix}5 & -8 \\\\ -6 & 3\\end{vmatrix}-8\\begin{vmatrix}-7 & -8 \\\\ 3 & 3\\end{vmatrix}+(-6)\\begin{vmatrix}-7 & 5 \\\\ 3 & -6\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =2(15-48)+(-8)(-21-(-24))+(-6)(42-15) \\\\  & =2\\cdot (-33)+(-8)\\cdot 3+(-6)\\cdot 27 \\\\  & =-66-24-162 \\\\  & =-252\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186534234 :question/problem "If $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 8 & -3 \\\\ 6 & 7 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -4\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ then $det(A)=${{field-1}}."] [17592186534234 :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}-2 & 8 & -3 \\\\ 6 & 7 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -2 & -4\\end{vmatrix} & =(-2)\\begin{vmatrix}7 & -3 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{vmatrix}-8\\begin{vmatrix}6 & -3 \\\\ 2 & -4\\end{vmatrix}+(-3)\\begin{vmatrix}6 & 7 \\\\ 2 & -2\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-2)(7\\cdot (-4)-(-3)\\cdot (-2))+(-8)(6\\cdot (-4)-(-3)\\cdot 2)+(-3)(6\\cdot (-2)-7\\cdot 2) \\\\  & =(-2)(-28-6)+(-8)(-24-(-6))+(-3)(-12-14) \\\\  & =(-2)\\cdot (-34)+(-8)\\cdot (-18)+(-3)\\cdot (-26) \\\\  & =68+144+78 \\\\  & =290\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186534237 :question/problem "If $$\n\\displaystyle Q=\\begin{bmatrix}-10 & -5 & 4 \\\\ -10 & 8 & -7 \\\\ -10 & -5 & -12\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ then $det(Q)=${{field-1}}."] [17592186534237 :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}-10 & -5 & 4 \\\\ -10 & 8 & -7 \\\\ -10 & -5 & -12\\end{vmatrix} & =(-10)\\begin{vmatrix}8 & -7 \\\\ -5 & -12\\end{vmatrix}-(-5)\\begin{vmatrix}-10 & -7 \\\\ -10 & -12\\end{vmatrix}+4\\begin{vmatrix}-10 & 8 \\\\ -10 & -5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-10)(8\\cdot (-12)-(-7)\\cdot (-5))+5((-10)\\cdot (-12)-(-7)\\cdot (-10))+4((-10)\\cdot (-5)-8\\cdot (-10)) \\\\  & =(-10)(-96-35)+5(120-70)+4(50-(-80)) \\\\  & =(-10)\\cdot (-131)+5\\cdot 50+4\\cdot 130 \\\\  & =1310+250+520 \\\\  & =2080\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186534244 :question/problem "If $$\n\\displaystyle Q=\\begin{bmatrix}8 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 9 & 8 & -4 \\\\ -12 & -9 & -10\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ then $det(Q)=${{field-1}}."] [17592186534244 :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}8 & 5 & 5 \\\\ 9 & 8 & -4 \\\\ -12 & -9 & -10\\end{vmatrix} & =8\\begin{vmatrix}8 & -4 \\\\ -9 & -10\\end{vmatrix}-5\\begin{vmatrix}9 & -4 \\\\ -12 & -10\\end{vmatrix}+5\\begin{vmatrix}9 & 8 \\\\ -12 & -9\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =8(8\\cdot (-10)-(-4)\\cdot (-9))+(-5)(9\\cdot (-10)-(-4)\\cdot (-12))+5(9\\cdot (-9)-8\\cdot (-12)) \\\\  & =8(-80-36)+(-5)(-90-48)+5(-81-(-96)) \\\\  & =8\\cdot (-116)+(-5)\\cdot (-138)+5\\cdot 15 \\\\  & =-928+690+75 \\\\  & =-163\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186534246 :question/problem "If $$\n\\displaystyle Q=\\begin{bmatrix}7 & -7 & 9 \\\\ -7 & -7 & 7 \\\\ 12 & -12 & 5\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ then $det(Q)=${{field-1}}."] [17592186534246 :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}7 & -7 & 9 \\\\ -7 & -7 & 7 \\\\ 12 & -12 & 5\\end{vmatrix} & =7\\begin{vmatrix}-7 & 7 \\\\ -12 & 5\\end{vmatrix}-(-7)\\begin{vmatrix}-7 & 7 \\\\ 12 & 5\\end{vmatrix}+9\\begin{vmatrix}-7 & -7 \\\\ 12 & -12\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =7((-7)\\cdot 5-7\\cdot (-12))+7((-7)\\cdot 5-7\\cdot 12)+9((-7)\\cdot (-12)-(-7)\\cdot 12) \\\\  & =7(-35-(-84))+7(-35-84)+9(84-(-84)) \\\\  & =7\\cdot 49+7\\cdot (-119)+9\\cdot 168 \\\\  & =343-833+1512 \\\\  & =1022\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186534251 :question/problem "If $$\n\\displaystyle W=\\begin{bmatrix}-9 & 9 & 8 \\\\ 12 & 8 & -5 \\\\ 6 & 5 & 8\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ then $det(W)=${{field-1}}."] [17592186534251 :question/worked-solution "Using the formula, we take the alternating sum of the entries in the top row of the matrix, multiplied by their respective minors, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\begin{vmatrix}-9 & 9 & 8 \\\\ 12 & 8 & -5 \\\\ 6 & 5 & 8\\end{vmatrix} & =(-9)\\begin{vmatrix}8 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 8\\end{vmatrix}-9\\begin{vmatrix}12 & -5 \\\\ 6 & 8\\end{vmatrix}+8\\begin{vmatrix}12 & 8 \\\\ 6 & 5\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =(-9)(8\\cdot 8-(-5)\\cdot 5)+(-9)(12\\cdot 8-(-5)\\cdot 6)+8(12\\cdot 5-8\\cdot 6) \\\\  & =(-9)(64-(-25))+(-9)(96-(-30))+8(60-48) \\\\  & =(-9)\\cdot 89+(-9)\\cdot 126+8\\cdot 12 \\\\  & =-801-1134+96 \\\\  & =-1839\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186534348 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) \\\\ 3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186534412 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) \\\\ -3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186534423 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) \\\\ -3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186534501 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) \\\\ -3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186534698 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}3\\operatorname{sinh}⁡(2t) \\\\ -3\\operatorname{cosh}⁡(2t)\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186534985 :question/problem "The function \n$f(x,y)$ \nhas the second derivative\n\n$$\n{f}^{″}(x,y)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2y & 4y-2x \\\\ 4y-2x & 4x\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nand has a stationary point at $(1,1).$ If $H$ denotes the Hessian determinant of $f,$ which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. $H(1,1)>0$\n\nII. $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,1)<0$\n\nIII. The point $(1,1)$ is a saddle point of $f$"] [17592186534985 :question/worked-solution "The second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(1,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,1)=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, let's examine our statements in turn.\n\n- Statement I is false. Computing the determinant of ${f}^{″}(1,1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(1,1)=det({f}^{″}(1,1))=(-2)\\cdot 4-{2}^{2}=-12<0.\n$$\n\n- Statement II is true. Indeed, we have $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(1,1)=({f}^{″}(1,1))_{11}=-2<0.$\n\n- Statement III is true. Indeed, since $H(1,1)<0,$ the point $(1,1)$ is a saddle point by the second partial derivative test.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""] [17592186535011 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=-5y(t) \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=5x(t),\\end{aligned}\\,\\begin{bmatrix}x(-2) \\\\ y(-2)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: $$\n\\displaystyle {e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5t)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ where $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"130px\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-2}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186535011 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ So, the matrix of the system is $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -5 \\\\ 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the general solution of the system can be written as\n\n$$\nx(t)=Φ(t)\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere $Φ(t)$ is a fundamental matrix of the system, and $c$ is a constant vector.\n\nSo, we can write our general solution as\n\n$$\nx(t)={Φ}_{0}(t)c={e}^{At}\\,c,\n$$\n\nwhere ${Φ}_{0}(t)={e}^{At}$ is the standard fundamental matrix of the system. Substituting $t={t}_{0},$ we have\n\n$$\nx({t}_{0})={e}^{A{t}_{0}}\\,c\n$$\n\nand since ${e}^{At}$ is invertible for all $t,$ we have\n\n$$\nc={e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}).\n$$\n\nPlugging the above back into our expression for $x(t),$ we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{At}\\,{e}^{-A{t}_{0}}\\,x({t}_{0}) \\\\  & ={e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0}).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the solution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & ={e}^{A(t+2)}\\,x(-2) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5(t+2)) & -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5(t+2)) \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡(5(t+2)) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(5(t+2))\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡(5(t+2))\\, \\\\ \\,\\operatorname{sin}⁡(5(t+2))\\,\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186535048 :question/worked-solution "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f′(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(x{y}^{2}-4y-4x) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(x{y}^{2}-4y-4x)\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}{y}^{2}-4 & 2(xy-2)\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting $f′(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{y}^{2}-4=0 \\\\ xy-2=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\n{y}^{2}-4=0\\,⟹\\,y=\\pm 2.\n$$\n\nThen, substituting these values into the second equation, we get\n\n$$\ny=-2\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{2}{-2}=-1,\\,\\,y=2\\,\\,\\,\\,\\,⟹\\,x=\\frac{2}{2}=1.\n$$\n\nHence, $(-1,-2)$ and $(1,2)$ are stationary points of $f(x,y).$\n\nTo classify these stationary points, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}({y}^{2}-4)=0\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2xy-4)=2y \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}({y}^{2}-4)=2y\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(2xy-4)=2x\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2y \\\\ 2y & 2x\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we evaluate the second derivative at each of the stationary points and use it to categorize them:\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(-1,-2),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(-1,-2)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -4 \\\\ -4 & -2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(-1,-2),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(-1,-2)=det({f}^{″}(-1,-2))=0\\cdot (-2)-(-4)^{2}=-16<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second partial derivative test, we have that $f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(-1,-2).$\n\n- Evaluating the second derivative at $(1,2),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(1,2)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(1,2),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(1,2)=det({f}^{″}(1,2))=0\\cdot 2-{4}^{2}=-16<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second partial derivative test, we have that $f(x,y)$ has a saddle point at $(1,2).$\n\nIn conclusion, $f(x,y)$ has saddle points at $(-1,-2)$ and $(1,2).$"] [17592186535054 :question/worked-solution "The local extrema of $f$ occur at its critical points. A point $a$ in the domain of $f$ is a critical point of $f$ if ${f}^{′}(a)={0}^{T}$ (for stationary points) or ${f}^{′}(a)$ doesn't exist.\n\nThe second partial derivative test states that if $a\\in {R}^{2}$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y)$ with Hessian determinant $H(a),$ we have the following:\n\n- If $H(a)>0,$ then:\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a local maximum at $a$\n\n- if $\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(a)>0,$ then $f$ has a local minimum at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)<0,$ then $f$ has a saddle point at $a$\n\n- If $H(a)=0,$ then the second partial derivatives test is inconclusive.\n\nFirst, we compute the first derivative of $f(x,y),$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f′(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂f}{∂x} & \\frac{∂f}{∂y}\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-2{x}^{2}+ey-{e}^{y}) & \\frac{∂}{∂y}(-2{x}^{2}+ey-{e}^{y})\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-4x & e-{e}^{y}\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe first derivative exists for any $(x,y)$ in the domain of the function, so we only need to find the stationary points. Setting $f′(x,y)={0}^{T},$ we obtain the following system:\n\n$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}-4x=0 \\\\ e-{e}^{y}=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSolving the first equation for $x,$ we get\n\n$$\n-4x=0\\,⟹\\,x=0.\n$$\n\nSolving the second  equation for $y,$ we get\n\n$$\ne-{e}^{y}=0\\,⟹\\,y=1.\n$$\n\nHence, $(0,1)$ is a stationary point of $f(x,y).$   \n\nTo classify this stationary point, we use the second partial derivative test.\n\nFirst, we compute the second partial derivatives:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(-4x)=-4\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} & =\\frac{∂}{∂x}(e-{e}^{y})=0 \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(-4x)=0\\, & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}} & =\\frac{∂}{∂y}(e-{e}^{y})=-{e}^{y}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, the second derivative  is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(x,y) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂x∂y} \\\\ \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂y∂x} & \\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{y}^{2}}\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -{e}^{y}\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEvaluating the second derivative at $(0,1),$ we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{f}^{″}(0,1)=\\begin{bmatrix}-4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -e\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nComputing the determinant $H(0,1),$ we get\n\n$$\nH(0,1)=det({f}^{″}(0,1))=(-4)\\cdot (-e)-{0}^{2}=4e>0.\n$$\n\nFinally, notice that \n\n$$\n\\frac{{∂}^{2}f}{∂{x}^{2}}(0,0)=({f}^{″}(0,1))_{11}=-4<0.\n$$\n\nTherefore, applying the second partial derivative test, we have that $f(x,y)$ has a local maximum at $(0,1).$"] [17592186535415 :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the curl of $F$ is the vector field given by\n\n$$\ncurl\\,F=∇\\times F=\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ P & Q & R\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ 8{z}^{2} & 2y & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =[\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2y)]i-[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(8{z}^{2})]j+[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(8{z}^{2})]k \\\\  & =(0-0)i-(0-16z)j+(0-0)k \\\\  & =16z\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186535416 :question/worked-solution "If $F=P\\,i+Q\\,j+R\\,k$ is a vector field on ${R}^{3}$ and the partial derivatives of the components of $F$ all exist, then the curl of $F$ is the vector field given by\n\n$$\ncurl\\,F=∇\\times F=\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ P & Q & R\\end{vmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\text{curl}\\,F & =\\begin{vmatrix}i & j & k \\\\ \\frac{∂}{∂x} & \\frac{∂}{∂y} & \\frac{∂}{∂z} \\\\ 7{z}^{2} & 2y & 1\\end{vmatrix} \\\\  & =[\\frac{∂}{∂y}(1)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(2y)]i-[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(1)-\\frac{∂}{∂z}(7{z}^{2})]j+[\\frac{∂}{∂x}(2y)-\\frac{∂}{∂y}(7{z}^{2})]k \\\\  & =(0-0)i-(0-14z)j+(0-0)k \\\\  & =14z\\,j.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186535950 :question/problem "$$\n\\{\\begin{aligned}{x}^{′}(t)=y(t)+1 \\\\ {y}^{′}(t)=-x(t),\\end{aligned}\\,\\begin{bmatrix}x(0) \\\\ y(0)\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nFind the solution to the initial value problem above.\n\n*Hint: $$\n\\displaystyle {e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\end{bmatrix},\n$$ where $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$*\n\n<table align=\"l\" cellpadding=\"0\" rules=\"all\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"30px\" rowspan=\"2\">$\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}=\\,\\,$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" height=\"40px\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"120px\">$2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t)$</td>\n<td align=\"center\" width=\"5px\"></td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n<td align=\"center\">{{field-1}}</td>\n<td align=\"center\"></td>\n</tr>\n</tbody></table>"] [17592186535950 :question/worked-solution "Writing our system in matrix form, we get\n\n$$\n{x}^{′}(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}x(t)+g(t),\n$$\n\nwhere $$\n\\displaystyle x(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}x(t) \\\\ y(t)\\end{bmatrix}\n$$ and $$\n\\displaystyle g(t)=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$ So, the matrix of the system is $$\n\\displaystyle A=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 \\\\ -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nGiven the system ${x}^{′}(t)=Ax(t)+g(t),$ where $A$ is a constant square matrix, the solution of the initial value problem can be written as the sum of the homogeneous part and a particular solution:\n\n$$\nx(t)=\\underbrace{{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})}\\,x({t}_{0})}_{\\text{homogeneous part}}+\\underbrace{{\\int }_{{t}_{0}}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s}_{\\text{particular solution}},\n$$\n\nwhere $x({t}_{0})$ is the initial condition vector. \n\nLet's evaluate each part separately:\n\n- First, we find the homogeneous part. We are given that \n\n$$\n{e}^{At}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSince the initial conditions are given at $t={t}_{0}=0,$ we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-{t}_{0})} & ={e}^{At}\\,x(0) \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- Next, we find the particular solution. Notice that \n\n$$\n{e}^{A(t-s)}=\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s)\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nSo, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s) & \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s)\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s)\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{t}{e}^{A(t-s)}g(s)\\,\\text{d}s & ={\\int }_{0}^{t}\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s) \\\\ -\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s)\\end{bmatrix}\\text{d}s \\\\  & ={\\begin{bmatrix}-\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t-s) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t-s)\\end{bmatrix}}_{s=0}^{s=t} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFinally, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}x(t) & =\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2\\operatorname{sin}⁡(t) \\\\ 2\\operatorname{cos}⁡(t)-1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186536351 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}34 & -17 \\\\ -17 & -29\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186536360 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}62 & -69 \\\\ -69 & 242\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186536366 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}25 & -25 \\\\ -25 & 47\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186536406 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}34 & 7 \\\\ 7 & -29\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186536409 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}52 & 30 \\\\ 30 & 250\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186536414 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -1 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186536431 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}65 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 340\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186536435 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186536440 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & 1 & 2 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186536447 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 42\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186536456 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 3 & -10\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186536463 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 42\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186536488 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186536499 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 5 \\\\ 5 & 42\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186536522 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 1 & 2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186536530 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}12 & 0 \\\\ 0 & -27\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186536547 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}46 & 77 \\\\ 77 & 21\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186536562 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186536585 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186536590 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 2 \\\\ 0 & 0 & -2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186536595 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 2 & -2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186536639 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 5 \\\\ 5 & -10\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186536667 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 1 & -2 \\\\ -3 & 1 & 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186536674 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 42\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186536692 :answer/value "\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}30 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 69\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186536822 :question/problem "$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 5 & -6 & 1 \\\\ 10 & -6 & 5 & -9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the observation matrix $B$ in the mean-deviation form above, where the individual observations are given in columns. \nFind the sample covariance matrix of the data."] [17592186536822 :question/worked-solution "Recall that  the sample covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nC=\\frac{1}{n-1}B{B}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is the observation matrix in the mean-deviation form, and $n$ is the number of observations.\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\frac{1}{4-1}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 5 & -6 & 1 \\\\ 10 & -6 & 5 & -9\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 10 \\\\ 5 & -6 \\\\ -6 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}62 & -69 \\\\ -69 & 242\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186536835 :question/problem "$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the observation matrix $B$ in the mean-deviation form above, where the individual observations are given in columns. \nFind the sample covariance matrix of the data."] [17592186536835 :question/worked-solution "Recall that  the sample covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nC=\\frac{1}{n-1}B{B}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is the observation matrix in the mean-deviation form, and $n$ is the number of observations.\n\nTherefore, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\frac{1}{4-1}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -1 & 1 & -2 \\\\ 0 & 5 & -1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 5 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -2 & -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}10 & 2 \\\\ 2 & 42\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186536836 :question/problem "The matrix $Q$ is given below. Let ${q}_{1}$, ${q}_{2}$,  ${q}_{3}$ and ${q}_{4}$ be the respective columns of $Q.$\nDetermine if $B=\\text{{}{q}_{1},{q}_{4}\\text{}}$ is a basis of $\\text{Col}(Q).$ \nIf that is the case, find ${x}_{1}\\cdot {x}_{4}$, where ${q}_{3}={x}_{1}{q}_{1}+{x}_{4}{q}_{4}.$ \n\n$$\nQ=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 0 & -30 & -15 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 6 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 24 & 14\\end{bmatrix}\n$$"] [17592186536836 :question/worked-solution "First, we reduce the matrix $Q$ to reduced row echelon form (RREF):\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Q & =\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & 0 & -30 & -15 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 6 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 24 & 14\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{1} & :=\\left(-\\frac{1}{5}\\right){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 6 & 3 \\\\ 4 & 0 & 24 & 14\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :={R}_{3}+(-4){R}_{1} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 3 & 6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{2} & :=\\frac{1}{3}{R}_{2} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix} & {R}_{3} & :=\\frac{1}{2}{R}_{3} \\\\  & ∼\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 6 & 3 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 2 & 1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFrom the matrix above, we see that the pivot columns are the $1$st, $2$nd, and $4$th columns. So, a basis of $\\text{Col}(Q)$ is $\\{{q}_{1},{q}_{2},{q}_{4}\\}.$ \n\nSo, we conclude that $B=\\{{q}_{1},{q}_{4}\\}$ is not a basis of $\\text{Col}(Q).$"] [17592186536842 :question/problem "$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 7 & 5 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the observation matrix $X$ above, where the individual observations are given in columns. Find the mean-deviation form of $X.$"] [17592186536842 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the mean-deviation form of the observation matrix\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | &  & | \\\\ {x}_{1} & {x}_{2} & ⋯ & {x}_{n} \\\\ | & | &  & |\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis given by \n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | &  & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & ⋯ & {b}_{n} \\\\ | & | &  & |\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere ${b}_{i}={x}_{i}-m$ for $i=1,2,…,n,$ and $m$ is sample mean vector.\n\nFrom the matrix, we have that  \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the sample mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{1}{3}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}-6 \\\\ 12\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & ={x}_{1}-m=\\begin{bmatrix}-5 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {b}_{2} & ={x}_{2}-m=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {b}_{3} & ={x}_{3}-m=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the mean-deviation form is\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -1 & 4 \\\\ 3 & 1 & -4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186536875 :question/problem "$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -2 & 3 \\\\ 7 & 5 & 9\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the observation matrix $X$ above, where the individual observations are given in columns. Find the mean-deviation form of $X.$"] [17592186536875 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the mean-deviation form of the observation matrix\n\n$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | &  & | \\\\ {x}_{1} & {x}_{2} & ⋯ & {x}_{n} \\\\ | & | &  & |\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nis given by \n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | &  & | \\\\ {b}_{1} & {b}_{2} & ⋯ & {b}_{n} \\\\ | & | &  & |\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nwhere ${b}_{i}={x}_{i}-m$ for $i=1,2,…,n,$ and $m$ is sample mean vector.\n\nFrom the matrix, we have that  \n\n$$\n{x}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix},\\,{x}_{3}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nAs a result, the sample mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{1}{3}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 21\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & ={x}_{1}-m=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {b}_{2} & ={x}_{2}-m=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {b}_{3} & ={x}_{3}-m=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 7\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the mean-deviation form is\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 & 2 \\\\ 0 & -2 & 2\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186536884 :question/problem "$$\nX=\\begin{bmatrix}4 & 6 & 5 \\\\ -3 & 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the observation matrix $X$ above, where the individual observations are given in columns. Find the sample covariance matrix of the data."] [17592186536884 :question/worked-solution "Recall that  the sample covariance matrix is given by\n\n$$\nC=\\frac{1}{n-1}B{B}^{T},\n$$\n\nwhere $B$ is the observation matrix in the mean-deviation form, and $n$ is the number of observations.\n\nSo, we first need to compute the mean-deviation form of our observation matrix.\n\nThe sample mean is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}m & =\\frac{1}{3}({x}_{1}+{x}_{2}+{x}_{3}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}(\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}+\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{3}\\begin{bmatrix}15 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNow, we have the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{b}_{1} & ={x}_{1}-m=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {b}_{2} & ={x}_{2}-m=\\begin{bmatrix}6 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\ {b}_{3} & ={x}_{3}-m=\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 5\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore,  the mean-deviation form is\n\n$$\nB=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nFinally, the sample covariance matrix is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\frac{1}{3-1}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -4 & -1 & 5\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{2}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 3 \\\\ 3 & 42\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186537244 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186537256 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186537359 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}5 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186537376 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 11\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186537500 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186537507 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186537559 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186537573 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186537575 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -3 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186537577 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186537588 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}2 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186537626 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186537735 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 4 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186537738 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ 0 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186537747 :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ -4 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}"] [17592186537904 :question/problem "Find the vector in the subspace spanned by $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ \nthat is closest to the vector $x,$ where \n\n$$\n{a}_{1}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ -4\\end{bmatrix},\\,{a}_{2}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186537904 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that ${a}_{1}$ and ${a}_{2}$ are linearly independent. \n\nThe closest vector to $x$ in the subspace spanned by $\\{{a}_{1},{a}_{2}\\}$ is orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace, which is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nIn our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ {a}_{1} & {a}_{2} \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,⇒\\,{A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & -4 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ -10 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}18 & -4 \\\\ -4 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-48 \\\\ 17\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{38}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 18\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-48 \\\\ 17\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 \\\\ 1 & -1 \\\\ -4 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -5 \\\\ 11\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186537968 :question/problem "Find the orthogonal projection of the vector $x$ onto the plane $r=sv+tw,$ where $s,t\\in R,$ and\n\n$$\nv=\\begin{bmatrix}3 \\\\ -2 \\\\ -2\\end{bmatrix},\\,w=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix},\\,x=\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186537968 :question/worked-solution "First, notice that $v$ and $w$ are linearly independent. \n\nThe orthogonal projection of $x$ onto the subspace $S$ spanned by the columns of a matrix $A$ is given by\n\n$$\n{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x=A({A}^{T}\\,A)^{-1}\\,{A}^{T}\\,x.\n$$\n\nSince our plane passes through the origin, it defines the subspace $S=\\text{Span}\\{v,w\\}$ in ${R}^{3}.$ In our case, we have\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}| & | \\\\ v & w \\\\ | & |\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\,⟹\\,{A}^{T}=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\nTherefore, we get the following projection:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\text{proj}}_{S}\\,x & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {(\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix})}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}3 & -2 & -2 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -6 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot {\\begin{bmatrix}17 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}}^{-1}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}16 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{50}\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ 1 & 17\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}16 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1 \\\\ -2 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 \\\\ -1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"] [17592186537969 :question/problem "$$\nU=\\begin{bmatrix}\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\end{bmatrix},\\,V=\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 & 1 \\\\ \\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0 \\\\ -\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}} & -\\frac{2}{\\sqrt{5}} & 0\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $U$ and $V$ above. Let $X$ be a $2\\times 3$ standardized observation matrix, where each row represents one observation and each column represents one feature. Given that $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $X,$ what is the third principal component of the data?"] [17592186537969 :question/worked-solution "Recall that the unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C$ are called the principal components of the data. If \n\n$$\n{\\lambda }_{1}>{\\lambda }_{2}>{\\lambda }_{3}\n$$\n\nare the eigenvalues of $C$ written in descending order, then: \n\n- the $1$st principal component is the unit eigenvector ${v}_{1}$ corresponding to the $1$st eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{1},$\n\n- the $2$nd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${v}_{2}$ corresponding to the $2$nd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{2},$\n\n- the $3$rd principal component is the unit eigenvector ${v}_{3}$ corresponding to the $3$rd eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{3}.$\n\nNotice that the covariance matrix corresponding to $X$ is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}C & =\\frac{1}{n-1}{X}^{T}X \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}(UΣ{V}^{T})^{T}(UΣ{V}^{T}) \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{n-1}V{Σ}^{T}({U}^{T}U)Σ{V}^{T} \\\\  & =V(\\frac{1}{n-1}{Σ}^{T}Σ){V}^{T},\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhere the final expression represents the diagonal form of the covariance matrix $C.$ Notice that:\n\n- $\\frac{1}{n-1}{Σ}^{T}Σ$ is a diagonal $3\\times 3$ matrix, where the entries on the main diagonal are the eigenvalues (written in descending order) of the covariance matrix $C$, and\n\n- $V$ is a $3\\times 3$ matrix, whose columns are the corresponding unit eigenvectors of the covariance matrix $C.$\n\nTherefore, the columns of $V$ are the principal components of the data.\nSo, the third principal component is \n\n$$\n\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"] [17592186537978 :question/problem "$$\nU=\\begin{bmatrix}| &  & | \\\\ {u}_{1} & ⋯ & {u}_{n} \\\\ | &  & |\\end{bmatrix},\\,V=\\begin{bmatrix}| &  & | \\\\ {v}_{1} & ⋯ & {v}_{m} \\\\ | &  & |\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nConsider the matrices $U$ and $V$ above. Let $X$ be an $n\\times m$ standardized observation matrix, where each row represents one observation and each column represents one feature. Given that $X=UΣ{V}^{T}$ is a singular value decomposition of $X,$ what is the second principal component of the data?"]}
